# Agent runtime
opencode.json
.opencode/

# Merge-assistant scratch — the conflict material handed to the agent, and its reply.
# Written mid-merge into a repo whose index is already occupied; never part of history.
.merge-assist/

# Parallel-worktree build dirs — each is its own checkout on its own branch (see
# workspace/git.py:create_worktree); never part of the root workspace's own tree.
.worktrees/

# Local database sandboxes. Their data directories contain generated binary files and
# must never become application source or a merge input.
.pg-sandbox/
.mssql-sandbox/
.mysql-sandbox/
.mongo-sandbox/
# The wave's shared authorship board. Worktrees cannot see each other's files, so two
# specs in one wave can each create the same service class, port or schema from nothing —
# and then merge with an EMPTY base, where git has no ancestor to reason from and the two
# designs simply contradict. This is the one file every worktree in a wave reads and
# appends to, so the second author extends the first instead of inventing a rival.
# Deliberately NOT tracked: it is coordination for a build in flight, not project content,
# and a tracked file in the shared tree would conflict in every merge the way KNOWLEDGE.md
# already does.
.wave/

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.local

