# Rust / Cargo
# (covered by target/ above)

# .NET / C#
# (a bare "bin/" is deliberately omitted — some ecosystems, e.g. Rails, commit
# scripts under bin/ (bin/rails, bin/setup); obj/ already covers most .NET
# intermediate build output unambiguously)
obj/
# The compiled output of a .NET project lives in bin/<Configuration>/, which IS unambiguous —
# unlike a bare bin/. A test project added by a LATER change is not covered by the per-project
# lines an agent appends, so two parallel specs each committed their own build of it
# (MiInventario.BLL.Tests/bin/Debug/net481/*.dll) and the wave merge died on binary conflicts.
**/bin/Debug/
**/bin/Release/
*.user
*.suo
.vs/
*.nupkg

# Ruby / Bundler
.bundle/
vendor/bundle/

# Coverage (generic)
coverage/
lcov.info
*.lcov

# Browser/E2E test output — the same family as coverage/ above, and missed for the same
# reason nobody notices build output until it collides. Playwright names its captures after
# the test, so two worktrees of one wave running the same suite write byte-different images
# to the SAME path: tracked, that is an unmergeable binary conflict on every wave merge.
# Nothing reads these from git — `test-results/` is only where captures are written and
# copied FROM (scan_screenshots archives them and records the archive's path), and
# `playwright-report/` is skipped by that scan outright.
test-results/
playwright-report/
blob-report/
# Verify flow captures at the workspace root. Archive copies remain versioned.
# Anchor this rule so docs/screenshots and application assets are still tracked.
/screenshots/

# Logs
*.log
logs/

# Temp / runtime
tmp/
*.tmp
*.pid
*.seed

# Databases a generated app runs locally. Postgres and friends drop a DATA DIRECTORY next
# to the app, and App Builder's own DB sandbox (run_command.py's four-engine support) drops
# one per engine at the workspace root — including a live Unix domain socket. That last one
# is not merely noise: `shutil.copytree` cannot copy a socket at all ([Errno 6]), which is
# why `workspace_clone.copy_ignore` already skips these by file type. Committing one is
# meaningless (it is a dead FD the moment the process exits) and a wave merge that tries to
# reconcile two of them has nothing to reconcile.
.pgrun/
.pg-run/
.pg_run/
.pglogs/
pgdata/
pg-data/
postgres-data/
postgres_data/
mysql-data/
mongo-data/
redis-data/
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite3-journal
dump.rdb

# Volumes a project mounts into its own tree
.docker-data/
docker-data/

# Nothing inside a source tree is build output.
#
# Every build-output rule above is a bare directory name, which git matches at ANY depth —
# so it reaches inside src/ too. `out/` is the worst of them: it is Next.js's static export
# directory, but `out` is also the outbound-port package of a ports-and-adapters layout, so
# `application/port/out/` and `adapters/out/**` were silently untracked. Dozens of .java
# files existed on disk and in no commit. They never travelled between a spec's worktree and
# Main, so each worktree grew its own copy, the wave merge had nothing to reconcile, and the
# damage only surfaced later as "cannot find symbol" in the merged-tree build gate — far from
# the .gitignore line that caused it. `bin/` was already left out of the .NET block for this
# same reason; these are the rest of the family.
#
# Re-including the DIRECTORY is what makes this work: git refuses to descend into an excluded
# directory, so a negation naming files inside it would do nothing, while a negation naming
# the directory itself makes git walk it again. Hence the trailing slash on every line.
#
# Deliberately per-name rather than a blanket `!**/src/**/`: caches DO legitimately live
# inside source trees (`src/pkg/__pycache__/`), and a blanket re-include would commit them.
#
# Source roots, not just src/: Go keeps its packages under internal/, pkg/ and cmd/, Rails
# under app/ and lib/, and each of those can hold an `out` package exactly as src/ can. This
# is a list of names, so it cannot be complete — a Python project laid out flat, with its
# packages directly under the repo root, has no root name to key on. Closing that last gap
# needs a check that looks at what git is actually hiding, not another pattern.
!**/src/**/out/
!**/src/**/build/
!**/src/**/dist/
!**/src/**/target/
!**/src/**/obj/
!**/internal/**/out/
!**/pkg/**/out/
!**/cmd/**/out/
!**/app/**/out/
!**/lib/**/out/

# The live preview's own environment file — written per snapshot, never the project's
preview/.env

# Agent Fabric pipeline
.env.local
.env.*.local
*.jks
*.keystore
key.properties
*.pyc
.npm-cache/
.skillcache/
*.tar

# Build output of the apps declared in openspec/config.yaml. Anchored to those
# paths on purpose: the same names loose in the tree match at any depth, which is
# how `out/` came to hide a ports-and-adapters `port/out/` package.
apps/mobile/out/
apps/mobile/dist/
apps/mobile/build/
apps/mobile/target/
apps/mobile/bin/
apps/mobile/obj/
apps/mobile/coverage/
